**user**:
> what is the etymology of ransack
**assistant**:
> The word "ransack" comes from the Old Norse word "rannsaka," which means to search a house. "Rann" translates as "house," and "saka" means "to seek." The term made its way into Middle English as "ransaken," maintaining the sense of thoroughly searching or plundering a place. Over time, it evolved into the Modern English "ransack." It's interesting how the term has maintained its core meaning of searching or plundering intensely throughout its history.
